---
import { getPullRequests } from '@src/utils/getPullRequests'
import Entry from './PullRequestListEntry.astro'
import css from './PullRequestsIndex.module.css'

const entries = await getPullRequests()

const featured = entries.filter((entry) => entry.data.featured)
const rest = entries.filter((entry) => !entry.data.featured)

// Dates are UTC midnight, so read them back in UTC too — see PullRequestArticle.astro.
const years = entries.map((entry) => entry.data.date.getUTCFullYear())
const timeSpan = `${Math.min(...years)} – ${Math.max(...years)}`

const wordedCount = new Intl.NumberFormat('en-GB').format(rest.length)
---

<div class={css.collection}>
    <header class={css.header}>
        <p class={css.kicker}>Portfolio · Writing</p>
        <h1>Pull Request Descriptions</h1>
        <p class={css.deck}>
            For me, a pull request is not just a formality. It's the place where actual teaching can happen: what
            changed, why it changed, how to review it, and what the team should do differently from now on.
        </p>
        <p class={css.deck}>
            I believe that each pull request deserves its own narrative, its own approach. Some require background and
            context, some require a detailed walkthrough of the code, some require a careful explanation of the
            trade-offs. I try to write each one in a way that will make it easy for the reviewer to understand and learn
            from.
        </p>
        <p class={css.deck}>Every pull request shown here was completely hand-written by me, without AI assistance.</p>
        <div class:list={[css.headerStats, css.mono]}>
            <span>{entries.length} selected</span>
            <span>{timeSpan}</span>
            <span>InterNations</span>
        </div>
    </header>

    <div class={css.intro}>
        <p>
            These are from the frontend platform work at <a href="https://www.internations.org/">InterNations</a>. It's
            a large single-page app carried through a long run of migrations: a global store, a router, a strict
            TypeScript flag, two major framework versions. Most of them were changes nobody had asked for and everybody
            would have to live with, which is a particular kind of writing problem: you have to earn the reviewer’s
            attention before you can spend it.
        </p>
        <p>
            The code is private and stays that way. What follows is the writing only, reproduced as it was published to
            the team, with internal links rendered as plain text.
        </p>
    </div>

    <ol class={css.entries}>
        {featured.map((entry) => <Entry entry={entry} />)}
    </ol>

    {
        rest.length > 0 && (
            <details class={css.more}>
                <summary class={css.moreSummary}>
                    {wordedCount} more
                    <span class={css.moreHint} aria-hidden="true">
                        {' '}
                        ↓
                    </span>
                </summary>
                {/* The counter keeps running so the two lists read as one collection */}
                <ol class={css.entries} style={`counter-reset: pr ${featured.length}`}>
                    {rest.map((entry) => (
                        <Entry entry={entry} />
                    ))}
                </ol>
            </details>
        )
    }

    <p class={css.outro}>
        Back to <a href="/portfolio/">the rest of the portfolio</a>.
    </p>
</div>
